OS Terminology:
===============
1) Services & Daemons:
----------------------
Background programs that run continuously without direct user interaction, performing tasks like managing hardware, providing network services, or handling scheduled jobs.

Services (Windows) vs Daemons (Linux):
---------------------------------------

Windows:                                                        
        Services 
=> Background programs called Services
=> Managed by the Windows Service Manager
=> Run without any user interaction
=> Examples: Windows Update, Print Spooler, Windows Defender

Linux:
        Daemons
=> Background processes called Daemons
=> Managed by systemd or init
=> Run without any user interaction
=> Examples: sshd, httpd, cron

2) Processes:
--------------
A process is an individual instance of a program currently in execution. Every running application — browser, terminal, antivirus — is a process. Each process is assigned a unique Process ID (PID) by the OS and can be monitored, started, stopped, or killed.
Windows:-
---------
=> Task Manager - GUI tool to view and manage running processes
=> tasklist - CMD command - list all running processes
=> taskkill - CMD command - terminate a process by PID or name

Linux:
------
=> ps - snapshot of current running processes
=> top / htop - Real-time live view of running processes and resource usage.
=> kill - Terminate a process using its PID

3) Threads:
------------
A thread is a sub-unit of a process. A single process can have multiple threads running concurrently — this is called multithreading. Threads share the same memory space as the parent process, enabling faster parallel execution of tasks within a program. Both Windows and Linux support multithreading.
Windows:
--------
=> Threads are managed within each process
=> Monitored through Task Manager

Linux:
------
=> Threads managed similarly within processes
=> Observed with ps -T or htop

Process vs Thread: A process is a full, independent program instance with its own memory. A thread is a lightweight unit of execution inside a process — multiple threads share the same memory and resources of their parent process.

4) File Permissions:
---------------------
Both Windows and Linux use file permissions to control what users can do with files — read, write, or execute. Permissions protect sensitive data and ensure only authorized users can access or modify system files.
Windows:
--------
-> Managed through the NTFS filesystem
-> Viewed via right-click -> Properties -> Security tab
-> Permissions: Full Control, Modify, Read & Execute, Read, Write

Linux:
------
-> Managed with chmod, chown, ls -l
-> Permissions split into: user, group, others
-> Permissions: read(r), write(w), execute (x)

5) Environment Variables:
--------------------------
Environment Variables are dynamic values stored in the OS that contain system and user-specific configurations — such as file paths, system settings, and application preferences. Programs use them to find resources without needing hardcoded paths.
windows:
---------
-> Set with the 'set' command in CMD.
-> Also configurable via System Properties -> Advanced -> Environment Variables
-> Key variable: %PATH% - directories where executables are found.

Linux:
-------
-> set with export VAR=value
-> View all variables with env
-> Persisted in ~/.bashrc or /etc/environment
-> Key variable: $PATH - same concept as Windows %PATH%
PATH is the most important environment variable — it tells the OS where to look for programs when you type a command. Without the correct PATH, the OS won't find your tools, scripts, or executables.

6) User Accounts & Permissions:
--------------------------------
Both OSes use user accounts to control who can do what on a system. There are different privilege levels — standard users have limited access, while administrators/root have full control.
Windows:
--------
-> Standard User: Limited access, no system changes
-> Administrator: Full system control
-> UAC (User Account Control): prompts for confirmation when actions require elevated privileges
-> Even admins must confirm via UAC prompt before elevated actions.

Linux:
-------
-> Standard User: Limited to own home directory and allowed resources
-> Root: The super user with unrestricted access to everything
-> sudo: temporarily elevate privileges for a single command without fully switching to root
-> su: switch to another user account entirely.

Security note for hackers: Privilege escalation — the act of gaining higher permissions than originally granted — is one of the most important post-exploitation techniques in offensive security. Understanding how Windows UAC and Linux sudo/root work is fundamental to this.
