Introduction to Cryptography:
=============================
Cryptography is the practice of securing information by transforming it into an unreadable format, allowing only authorised users to convert it back into a readable format.

1) Purpose:
------------
=> Confidentiality - Information is accessible only to those authorised to view it.
=> Integrity - Protects data from being altered or tampered with.
=> Authentication - Verifies the identity of users and systems involved in communication.
=> Non-repudiation - Senders cannot deny sending a message; recipients cannot deny receiving it.

2) Basic Terminology:
----------------------
=> Plaintext - The original, readable data before encryption is applied.
=> Ciphertext - The encrypted, unreadable data after a cryptographic algorithm is applied.
=> Key - A piece of information used in the encryption and decryption process, essential for securing data.

Plaintext + key => Algorithm => Ciphertext => Key => Plaintext

3) Types of cryptography - overview:
-------------------------------------
There are 3 main types. Each serves a different purpose in securing data:
=> Symmetric Cryptography - Uses the same key for both encryption and decryption. Example: AES (Advanced Encryption Standard).
=> Asymmetric Cryptography - Uses a pair of keys — one public, one private — for encryption and decryption. Example: RSA (Rivest-Shamir-Adleman).
=> Hash Functions - Algorithms that transform data into a fixed-size string of characters. One-way only — cannot be reversed. Example: SHA-256 (Secure Hash Algorithm).

4) Symmetric cryptography - in depth:
--------------------------------------
Same key for encrypt + decrypt
Both the sender and recipient share the same key. The sender encrypts the plaintext with the key to produce ciphertext; the recipient uses the same key to decrypt it back to plaintext.

Example Algorithm: AES — Advanced Encryption Standard

Sender -> Plaintext -> Shared key -> ciphertext -> Shared key -> Decrypted plaintext -> Recipient

Key characteristics:
=> Speed - Generally faster than asymmetric encryption and requires less computational power — efficient for large amounts of data.
=> Weakness - Requires secure sharing and storage of the single key. If the key is intercepted, anyone can decrypt the data.
=> Common use cases - Encrypting bulk data in storage (e.g., database encryption) and during data transfer within secure channels.

5) Asymmetric cryptography - in depth:
---------------------------------------
Public key encrypts · private key decrypts
Uses a pair of keys. The public key is shared openly and used to encrypt data. The private key is kept secret and used to decrypt it. Only the recipient who holds the private key can read the message.

Example Algorithm: RSA — Rivest-Shamir-Adleman

Sender -> Plaintext -> Public key -> Ciphertext -> Private key -> Decrypted plaintext -> Recipient

Key characteristics:
=> Speed - Slower and more resource-intensive due to complex mathematical operations. Best suited for smaller data sizes or for securely exchanging symmetric keys.
=> Security Advantage - More secure for data exchange — only the public key needs to be shared, while the private key remains confidential. The two-key system reduces the risk associated with key exchange.
=> Common use cases - Secure key exchange, digital signatures, and certificates, as well as establishing secure connections (e.g. SSL/TLS).

6) Hash functions - in depth:
------------------------------
One-way · no key · fixed output
Algorithms that take any input and transform it into a fixed-size string of characters. Unlike encryption, hashing is irreversible — you cannot get the original data back from a hash.

Example Algorithm: SHA-256 — Secure Hash Algorithm (always produces a 256-bit hash)

Plaintext (any size) -> SHA-256 hash function -> f7ff9e8b7b2e09b709…

3 Core Properties:
=> One-way function - Hashing is irreversible. Once data is transformed into a hash, it is practically impossible to revert it to its original form.
=> Fixed output size - Regardless of the size of the input data, hashing always produces a fixed-length output. SHA-256 always produces a 256-bit hash — useful for data integrity checks.
=> Deterministic - The same input will always produce the same hash output. Even a tiny change in the input yields a completely different hash — critical for verifying data integrity.

           Property                        Symmetric                                Asymmetric                                        Hashing
           --------                        ----------                               -----------                                       -------
1)         Keys used                       1 shared key                             Public + private pair                             no key
2)         Reversible?                     Yes (with key)                           Yes (with private key)                            No - One-way only
3)         Speed                                Fast                                       Slow                                       Very fast
4)         Data size                       Good for bulk data                        Best for small data/key exchange                 Any size -> fixed output
5)         Main risk                       Key interception                          Resource-intensive                               Cannot decrypt - integrity only
6)         Use cases                       Database encryption, secure channels      SSL/TLS, digital signatures, certificates        Password storage, data integrity checks
7)         Example                                 AES                                          RSA                                                SHA-256

 Hacker relevance: Understanding cryptography is essential for offensive security — cracking weak keys (symmetric), exploiting poor key management (asymmetric), and identifying hash collisions are all real attack vectors.
