Introduction to Shell commands & Scripting:
===========================================
1) What are shell commands?
----------------------------
Shell commands are specific instructions entered into a CLI or terminal that control the operating system or interact with files and applications. Unlike GUIs (which use buttons and icons), CLIs use text commands for operations.
Purpose: Allow quick execution of routine tasks and control of the computer without a graphical interface.

CLI vs Terminal — what's the difference?
CLI / Shell: The interface itself — you type text commands to perform tasks. No graphical elements. Examples: Bash, PowerShell.
Terminal: The application that gives you access to the CLI. Think of it as the door. Examples: Windows Terminal, GNOME Terminal, Terminal.app (macOS), Command Prompt.

For offensive security, a CLI or Terminal provides a method to control the operating system and create scripts. Most hacking tools run purely from the CLI.

2) What is scripting?
----------------------
Writing code to automate tasks. Scripts combine multiple commands with logic (loops, conditionals, functions) to automate workflows, perform calculations, and manipulate data efficiently — more powerful than individual commands.
Scripts are used to:
=> Save time - Automate repetitive tasks instead of typing them manually each time.
=> Reduce errors - Automated workflows are consistent — no typos or missed steps.
=> Simplify complexity - Complex multi-step tasks become a single concise script.

3) Basic CLI concepts:
-----------------------
=> Prompt - A symbol indicating the system is ready to receive commands. Shows the current directory and username.
$ in Unix/Linux and > in Windows
=> Commands - Instructions that tell the computer to perform specific actions. Each follows a specific syntax that may include options and arguments.

4) Common command-line operations
----------------------------------
Navigating directories:-
=> cd [directory] - Change to a specified directory
=> pwd - Print the current working directory — shows your location in the file system

Listing files:-
=> ls - Show files and folders in the current directory (Linux/Mac)
=> dir - Show files and folders in the current directory (Windows)

Creating and managing files & directories:-
=> mkdir [dir] - Create a new directory
=> touch [file] - Create a new file (Linux/Mac)
=> echo. > [file] - Create a new file (Windows)
=> rm [file] - Delete a file — irreversible, use with caution!
=> rmdir [dir] - Remove an empty directory

Flags and parameters:-
Commands accept additional options called flags to modify behaviour.
Example: ls -l — provides a detailed list including permissions, ownership, and file size.

Command history and autocompletion:-
Command history - Use ↑ / ↓ arrow keys to navigate previously entered commands. Saves time and increases efficiency.
Autocompletion - Press Tab to autocomplete commands or file names. Reduces typographical errors and speeds up entry.

Redirecting and piping:-
cmd > file.txt  => Save command output to a file. Example: ls > filelist.txt -> this will save the list of files in the current directory to the filelist.txt
cmd1 | cmd2 => Pipe output of one command into another. Example: ls | grep 'txt' -> this lists only the text files from the current directory

Getting help:-
man [command] => Open manual page for a command — detailed info (Linux)
[cmd] --help => Quick info about command usage and options

5) Common scripting languages:
-------------------------------
=> Python - Readable, widely used across fields.Use cases: Data analysis, automation, web scraping.
=> Bash - Unix/Linux system tasks.Use cases: File management, backups, system monitoring.
=> CMD - Default Windows shell. Use cases: Basic file ops, batch file creation, network diagnostics.
=> PowerShell - Powerful Windows scripting (cross-platform via PS Core). Use cases: Task automation, configuration management.

Differences:-
Property                      Bash                                Python                               CMD                        Powershell
Syntax                     Concise, Unix-focused                General-purpose                     Simple commands              Object-oriented
OS                         Unix/Linux                           All platforms                       Windows                      Windows (+ cross-platform)
Best for                   System-level tasks                   Versatile (web, data science)       System-level tasks           Windows system management

Similarities:-
=> Automation focus - All four can automate tasks like file manipulation and system monitoring.
=> Control structures - All support loops and conditionals.
=> File operations - Each can create, delete, and modify files — syntax differs.
=> Environment access - All access environment variables and manage OS resources.

6) Environment variables & PATH:
---------------------------------
Variables like PATH store system paths for quick command access. Commonly used in scripts to define file paths and configuration settings.

Windows search order (when searching for a file or application):-
Current directory of the binary -> current directory of the user -> System directories -> Windows directory -> Current directory of the user -> PATH folders

If the right folders are not included in PATH, your program will not be found. PATH is a vital system variable — understanding it is essential for tool setup and privilege escalation research.

7) Basic script syntax - variables, data types, comments:
----------------------------------------------------------
variables - storing reusable values. Example: name="John"
        Python:      message = "Hello, World!"
        Bash:        message="Hello, World!"
        CMD:         set message="Hello, World!"
        PowerShell:  $message = "Hello, World!"

Data types - Simple types like string, int, and boolean, allowing scripts to handle text, numbers, and logic.
        String - A sequence of characters that represents text. Often used for storing and manipulating textual data. Example: "Hello, world!" or 'Python'
        Integer - A whole number, which means a numeric value without decimals. Can be both positive and negative. Example: 42 or -7
        Float - Represents numbers with decimal points. Often used for precision in calculations. Example: 3.14 or -0.001
        boolean - Can hold only two values: True or False. Used in logical conditions and control flow. Example: True or False
        List (or Array) - A structured collection of elements that can contain different data types. Often dynamic - can add or remove elements. Example: [1,2,3,4] or ['apple','banana','cherry']
        Dictionary (or hashmap) - Collection of key-value pairs, where each key is unique and corresponds to a value. Used to store associated data. Example: {'name':'Alice','age':30,'city':'Amsterdam'}

Comments - Notes within code, ignored by the interpreter, improve readability. Example: # This is a comment in Python
        Python: # This is a single-line comment.
                " " "
                This is a
                multi-line comment
                " " "
        Bash: # This is a single-line comment
        CMD: REM This is a single-line comment
        Powershell: # This is a single-line comment
                    <#
                    This is a
                    multi-line comment
                    #>

8) Control Structures:
-----------------------
=> Conditionals: if-else statements execute code based on specific conditions. Bash example: if [ "$age" -gt 18 ]; then echo "Adult"; fi
=> Loops: Repeat tasks with for or while loops. Bash example: for i in {1..5}; do echo $i; done
=> Functions: Group code blocks to reuse logic. Python example: def greet(): print("Hello!")

9) Putting it together - full Python script example
This script from the course combines functions, conditionals, input, and data types. Read through it and try to understand each part.

# Function to greet the user and check voting eligibility
def greet_user(name, age):
    # Print a greeting message
    print(f"Hello, {name}! Welcome to our program.")

    # Check if the user is eligible to vote
    if age >= 18:
        print("You are eligible to vote!")
    else:
        print("You are not eligible to vote yet.")

# Main program execution starts here
if __name__ == "__main__":
    # Prompt the user for their name
    user_name = input("Please enter your name: ")   # String

    # Prompt the user for their age
    user_age = input("Please enter your age: ")      # String input

    # Convert the age to an integer
    user_age = int(user_age)                          # Integer conversion

    # Call the function with user input
    greet_user(user_name, user_age)

Concepts used in this script:
-> Function definition: def greet_user(name, age) — defines a reusable block with parameters.
-> Conditional: if age >= 18 — branches execution based on a condition.
-> Input & data types: input() always returns a String. int() converts it to an Integer for comparison.
-> Entry point: if __name__ == "__main__" — ensures the main block only runs when executed directly.
