Setting up my Homelab:
======================
A self-hosted virtualized environment used to safely practice offensive security techniques. All machines run as virtual machines (VMs) on your own hardware — no real systems are harmed.

1) The lab simulates a real-world network with 3 zones:-
------------------------------------------------------
=> External zone - Attacker machines. Simulates the "outside" — the perspective of a hacker entering the network.
=> DMZ Zone - Demilitarized zone. Public-facing servers (web, vulnerable Linux). Buffer between external and internal.
=> Internal zone - Corporate LAN. Active Directory, servers, and client workstations are the most sensitive area.

DMZ - A buffer zone between a private internal network and untrusted external networks (like the internet). It hosts public-facing services with restricted external access, while isolating them from the internal network. This limits direct exposure of the internal zone to external threats.

2) Lab nodes - the 8 virtual machines:
--------------------------------------
a) External zone — attacker machines:-
=> Kali Linux - Primary attacker machine. One of the most popular Linux distros for penetration testing. Preloaded with tools for scanning, exploiting, and analysing network vulnerabilities.
=> Commando VM - Windows attacker machine. A Windows-based attack machine used for Windows-specific offensive tasks and tools.

b) DMZ zone — intentionally vulnerable targets:-
=> Metasploitable - Vulnerable Linux machine. An intentionally vulnerable Linux distro by Rapid7. Used to practice exploitation of known vulnerabilities and attack techniques.
=> bWAPP - Vulnerable web server. A PHP-based vulnerable web app with 100+ vulnerabilities. Great for both basic and advanced web application attack practice.
=> pfSense - Firewall (DMZ ↔ Internal). An open-source firewall and router sits between the DMZ and the internal network. Used to understand firewall rules, bypass techniques, and network segmentation

c) Internal zone - corporate network:-
=> Server 1 - Domain Controller — Windows Server 2019. Runs Active Directory + Certificate Services. Core of the internal network — manages identity, authentication, and group policy.
=> Server 2 - IIS + SQL Server — Windows Server 2019. Runs Microsoft IIS (web server) and SQL. Used to practice web and database attacks in a Windows environment.
=> 2x Clients - Windows 10 / 11 workstations. Joined the Active Directory domain. Used to practice lateral movement, privilege escalation, and Windows client exploitation.

3) Hardware & Software pre-requisites:
---------------------------------------
=> RAM - 16 GB minimum — more is better for running multiple VMs simultaneously.
=> Processor - 8-core CPU
=> Disk space - 100 GB minimum
=> Licences - Windows licence keys required. Windows Server 2019 has a 180-day trial.

